WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:03.460
Everyone knows that work is lame.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.920
Even if you're an actor!

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.460
Sometimes all the fame and money that come
with a big role don't seem worth enduring

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.519
a cruel director or a miserable film set.

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:17.910
We've already spotlighted a few times where
one scene nearly caused an actor to walk away,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.619
but it's apparently a lot more common than
you'd think.

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:24.860
We explored some maddening moments that drove
their actors crazy in our earlier video, Scenes

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.240
That Almost Caused The Actors to Quit.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.210
Well, we're now back with more scenes
that almost pushed actors right over the edge...

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.589
Daisy Ridley in Star Wars: The Force Awakens

00:00:35.589 --> 00:00:39.500
Starring in one of the most anticipated movies
of the century is an opportunity most actors

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.760
would kill for.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.930
But the pressure of filming 2015's Star Wars:
The Force Awakens nearly proved too much for

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.260
Daisy Ridley.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.440
The British newcomer didn't have much experience
before J.J. Abrams cast her as Rey, and Screen

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.960
Rant reports she almost quit on the first
day when he told her that her performance

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.420
during a desert scene felt "wooden."

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.360
That note, along with bearing the weight of
the biggest franchise in movie history, almost

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.650
prompted Ridley to get on a spaceship to anywhere
far, far away.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.010
Shelley Duvall in The Shining

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.870
Shelley Duvall set a Guinness World Record
for her portrayal of Wendy in Stanley Kubrick's

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:18.780
1980 horror classic The Shining – but it's
not a record anyone would want.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.530
The perfectionist Kubrick insisted on 127
takes of the scene where Duvall's hysterical

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.410
character uses a bat to fend off a deranged
Jack Nicholson.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:33.320
By the end, she so was exhausted and dehydrated
from crying that she nearly quit.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.240
She didn't, but the stress of filming the
scene was bad enough that she reportedly began

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.640
losing her hair.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.300
Zoe Saldana in Pirates of the Caribbean

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.229
Before she landed leading roles in huge blockbusters
like Avatar and Guardians of the Galaxy, Saldana

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:49.850
was just another struggling actress getting
by on bit parts.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.721
She had a small one as Anamaria in 2003's
Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of the

00:01:53.721 --> 00:01:57.830
Black Pearl, and she said she was treated
so badly that she nearly quit, telling The

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:58.840
Hollywood Reporter,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.150
"It was very elitist.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.790
People disrespecting me because they look
at my number on a call sheet and they think

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.170
I'm not important."

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:09.950
It was so demoralizing, Saldana said she even
considered quitting acting altogether.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.470
“Anna Maria.”

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.370
“I suppose you didn’t deserve that one
either.”

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:16.360
“No, that one I deserve.”

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.060
Mike Myers in Wayne's World

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.950
Mike Myers created the character of Wayne
Campbell on Saturday Night Live, and the recurring

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.870
sketch became so popular that Paramount wanted
a Wayne's World movie.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.340
Myers wrote the screenplay, and he was especially
excited about the scene where Wayne and the

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:34.150
boys pack into the Mirthmobile and headbang
to Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody."

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.780
But the studio wanted to replace the song
with something fresher and cheaper to license.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.720
Myers thought the Queen song was crucial,
as he revealed on WTF with Marc Maron.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.850
“At one point I said to everybody well I’m
out.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.430
I don’t want to make this movie if its not
Bohemian Rhapsody.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.210
And they were like, who the Fu*k are you?”

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:50.210
“Yeah.”

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.800
“I said, I’m somebody that… that wants
to do that movie.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.970
That’s the movie I want to do.”

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.800
Both Myers and Bohemian Rhapsody stayed.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.270
Gene Hackman in The French Connection

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:05.500
Gene Hackman won an Oscar for his turn as
New York City police detective Jimmy "Popeye"

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.900
Doyle in 1971's The French Connection, but
it almost didn't happen.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.830
A notoriously difficult actor, he often clashed
with director William Friedkin, especially

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:18.770
after Friedkin revealed Paul Newman was his
first choice for the part.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:22.459
Things came to a head during a scene where
Hackman – dressed in a Santa Claus costume

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:24.880
– tackles a criminal on the street.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.020
Friedkin demanded 27 takes, and Hackman nearly
quit.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:33.680
Fortunately, he stayed and even mentioned
it in his speech at the Academy Awards.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.890
John Cena in 12 Rounds

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.780
Few wrestling stars are bigger than John Cena,
so when WWE produced the direct to video cop

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.680
thriller 12 Rounds in 2009, casting him as
the lead was a no brainer.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.270
Although his day job is to battle hulking
beasts in the ring, Cena apparently isn't

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.410
fearless.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.590
One scene called for his character to rappel
down a skyscraper and dangle mid-air from

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.590
a rope.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:01.300
But Cena revealed later that his severe fear
of heights triggered intense anxiety and he

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.879
considered walking away before he ever got
up there.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:07.680
Mary Elizabeth Mastrantonio in The Abyss

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.460
The dark, claustrophobic sets and constantly
gushing water made for some difficult days

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.840
on the set of James Cameron's 1989 sci-fi
flick The Abyss.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.479
But one day in particular stands out for Mary
Elizabeth Mastrantonio.

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:24.949
For a scene in which her character's estranged
husband administers her CPR, Cameron asked

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:31.009
co-star Ed Harris to frantically slap Mastrantonio
as she lay motionless on the cold, wet floor.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:34.120
Then the camera ran out of film and enough
was enough.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.529
“And she hear that and she just like freaked.

00:04:38.529 --> 00:04:40.110
She just said hey man, we are not animals.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.400
I don’t know what you are doing here but
we are not animals.”

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.800
After leaving the set, she eventually calmed
down and returned.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.550
Carl Weathers in Rocky IV

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:54.039
A big part of the fourth Rocky movie involves
Rocky's mentor, Apollo Creed, staging a comeback.

00:04:54.039 --> 00:04:58.520
Played by former NFL linebacker Carl Weathers,
Creed gets roughed up in the ring pretty bad

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.050
by Communist pugilist Ivan Drago, played by
Dolph Lundgren.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.080
But one of the boxing scenes got a little
too real when Lundgren threw Weathers into

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.960
a corner, causing Weathers to break character
and throw a real punch.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.259
Lundgren responded in kind, knocking Weathers
out.

00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:17.900
When he regained consciousness, he quit the
movie and sent production into limbo for four

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:18.900
days.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.610
That's when Sylvester Stallone finally convinced
Weathers to return.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.479
Tippi Hedren in The Birds

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.930
Alfred Hitchcock had a reputation for being
unkind to actresses, but Tippi Hedren signed

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:33.529
on to 1963's The Birds under the guarantee
that she'd only be attacked with mechanical

00:05:33.529 --> 00:05:36.440
birds during the film's climactic scene.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.279
But Hitchcock is the king of twists, and he
wanted Hedren's terror to feel real, so he

00:05:41.279 --> 00:05:45.400
instructed crew members to throw real, live
birds at her.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:52.009
This real-life game of Angry Birds went on
for five full days and Hedren had a breakdown

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:53.469
when it was over.

00:05:53.469 --> 00:05:57.770
She only finished the movie when a doctor
ordered Hitchcock to give her a week off to

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:58.770
recover.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:05:59.770
Thanks for watching!

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.449
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:05.309
And leave us a comment to let us know which
scene would have made you quit...

